1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,864
The repository is repaired.
The check script would pass.

2
00:00:07,904 --> 00:00:12,849
You're tired, and the CTO, the chief
technology officer, stops you in the corridor

3
00:00:12,889 --> 00:00:17,652
with those two questions. You begin to
explain the interactive rebase, the todo

4
00:00:17,692 --> 00:00:22,272
list, the lease: the Git details of the
repair. After thirty seconds the CTO

5
00:00:22,312 --> 00:00:24,886
interrupts: "I did not
ask what you typed."

6
00:00:25,226 --> 00:00:29,702
A CTO reads an incident summary to decide
three things: whether customers or

7
00:00:29,742 --> 00:00:34,396
outsiders are affected, whether anything
is still at risk, and whether to spend

8
00:00:34,436 --> 00:00:38,615
money or attention on prevention.
Commands don't help with any of them.

9
00:00:38,655 --> 00:00:42,715
Hold on to those two questions. Each
has its own part of the summary.

10
00:00:44,555 --> 00:00:48,027
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:52,805
The last nine videos were about the repository.
This one is about what remains when

12
00:00:52,845 --> 00:00:56,720
the repository is fine: the summary,
the postmortem and the control.

13
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:01,498
The textbook calls these the parts that are
not Git, and they're the parts by which

14
00:01:01,538 --> 00:01:04,492
other people judge whether
the incident was handled.

15
00:01:04,832 --> 00:01:09,744
Three subjects. The four-part summary, and
the one thing that must never be claimed

16
00:01:09,784 --> 00:01:14,518
in its third part. The blameless postmortem,
with a template, and the reason why

17
00:01:14,558 --> 00:01:17,979
"blameless" is a technical
requirement and not a courtesy.

18
00:01:18,019 --> 00:01:22,991
And the step from a root cause to a control,
with a ranking of controls by strength.

19
00:01:23,331 --> 00:01:28,186
There are no new commands. The terminal
segment builds a timeline, because a summary

20
00:01:28,226 --> 00:01:32,907
and a postmortem are only as good as the
timeline under them, and every line of a

21
00:01:32,947 --> 00:01:34,306
timeline needs a source.

22
00:01:36,146 --> 00:01:37,724
Here's what you'll walk away with.

23
00:01:38,064 --> 00:01:39,476
After this video you can:

24
00:01:39,816 --> 00:01:44,576
Write the four-part incident summary and
say what must never be claimed in its third

25
00:01:44,616 --> 00:01:44,862
part.

26
00:01:45,202 --> 00:01:48,820
Build a timeline in which every
line names the source of its fact.

27
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:51,621
Write a blameless postmortem
with the template.

28
00:01:51,961 --> 00:01:56,570
Turn a root cause into a control and
classify it as prevent, detect or recover.

29
00:01:56,910 --> 00:01:58,905
Say how you know a control works.

30
00:02:00,745 --> 00:02:04,207
The summary. Four parts, in this
order, fitting on one screen.

31
00:02:04,547 --> 00:02:08,877
The third part carries the rule of this
video: never write that something was

32
00:02:08,917 --> 00:02:12,851
verified unless you ran the check.
And state what is not yet verified.

33
00:02:12,891 --> 00:02:17,505
A summary that claims more than was checked
fails at the first follow-up question,

34
00:02:17,545 --> 00:02:20,116
and after that nothing
else in it is believed.

35
00:02:20,456 --> 00:02:23,707
And there are the CTO's two
questions from the corridor.

36
00:02:23,747 --> 00:02:28,056
Part three answers "is it over". Part
four answers "will it happen again".

37
00:02:28,396 --> 00:02:33,472
Five habits make a summary trustworthy.
Separate what you observed from what you

38
00:02:33,512 --> 00:02:38,588
infer, and say which is which. Give every
time and every ID a source: the reflog

39
00:02:38,628 --> 00:02:43,767
entry, the tag, the log line. Say "I do
not know yet" early, with the time of the

40
00:02:43,807 --> 00:02:44,535
next update.

41
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:48,680
Never write that something was
verified unless you ran the check.

42
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:53,590
And send a first, short version before the
recovery, as soon as the team knows what

43
00:02:53,630 --> 00:02:58,441
to leave alone: silence during an incident
is read as "it is worse than they say".

44
00:02:58,781 --> 00:03:03,884
A status without a root cause is still a
status. The same four parts, one sentence

45
00:03:03,924 --> 00:03:07,648
each, are the spoken answer when
the CTO asks in a corridor.

46
00:03:07,988 --> 00:03:12,311
The postmortem, in one sentence. A postmortem
is a written record of an incident

47
00:03:12,351 --> 00:03:16,402
that explains how the system allowed
it, so that the system can be changed.

48
00:03:16,442 --> 00:03:20,711
"Blameless" means it treats every person's
action as reasonable given what they

49
00:03:20,751 --> 00:03:20,984
knew.

50
00:03:21,324 --> 00:03:26,430
Why blameless is a technical requirement.
The evidence in the last nine videos came

51
00:03:26,470 --> 00:03:31,575
from people: Ravi's reflog, his clone's
local record of where each branch has been,

52
00:03:31,615 --> 00:03:36,101
Asha's account of the reset, a developer
saying "I committed a password".

53
00:03:36,141 --> 00:03:41,309
People who expect blame run git gc, Git's
clean-up, or re-clone, or stay silent, and

54
00:03:41,349 --> 00:03:42,611
the evidence is gone.

55
00:03:42,951 --> 00:03:47,394
The textbook cites Google's SRE book, as
a secondary source, for the principle of

56
00:03:47,434 --> 00:03:51,878
assuming that everyone involved "had good
intentions and did the right thing with

57
00:03:51,918 --> 00:03:56,362
the information they had". The useful
question is never "who ran the command" but

58
00:03:56,402 --> 00:03:59,739
"why did running it look right,
and why did nothing stop it".

59
00:04:00,079 --> 00:04:05,228
The test. Replace every name by a role. If
the document still explains the incident,

60
00:04:05,268 --> 00:04:09,615
it's about the system. "Ravi force-pushed
production" explains nothing.

61
00:04:09,655 --> 00:04:14,681
"The production branch accepted a forced
update from any member with write access"

62
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:16,905
names something that can be changed.

63
00:04:17,245 --> 00:04:21,865
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds.
Write one sentence about a mistake you

64
00:04:21,905 --> 00:04:26,831
remember at work, with a name in it. Then
replace the name by a role, and say out

65
00:04:26,871 --> 00:04:29,160
loud what the sentence still explains.

66
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,513
If it explains nothing now, that's
normal: the sentence was about a person.

67
00:04:38,553 --> 00:04:43,127
The next draft asks why the action looked
right, and why nothing stopped it.

68
00:04:43,467 --> 00:04:47,357
Write the postmortem within days,
while reflogs and memories exist.

69
00:04:47,397 --> 00:04:52,225
Have the people involved review the timeline.
An action without an owner and a date

70
00:04:52,265 --> 00:04:56,918
is a wish. A postmortem whose only action
is "be more careful" has not found the

71
00:04:56,958 --> 00:04:57,270
cause.

72
00:04:57,610 --> 00:05:02,556
From root cause to control. A control is
something that still works when the person

73
00:05:02,596 --> 00:05:07,602
is new, tired or in a hurry. Controls differ
in strength, and the order of the table

74
00:05:07,642 --> 00:05:09,885
is the order in which to look for one.

75
00:05:10,225 --> 00:05:13,190
Four questions take you
from root cause to control.

76
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:18,140
Which layer could have refused the action:
the platform, the pipeline, the client, a

77
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:22,912
reviewer? What is the strongest control
available on that layer and on your plan,

78
00:05:22,952 --> 00:05:27,567
since some ruleset features are plan-gated?
What will the control block that is

79
00:05:27,607 --> 00:05:32,339
legitimate, and what is the path for that
case? And how will you know it works: a

80
00:05:32,379 --> 00:05:36,935
ruleset's insights, a test that fails
when the fix is removed, an attempt in a

81
00:05:36,975 --> 00:05:37,406
sandbox.

82
00:05:37,746 --> 00:05:43,484
Quick quiz. A release job tests the ancestry
of a branch and refuses to deploy a

83
00:05:43,524 --> 00:05:48,394
rewritten one. Is that control A,
prevent, B, detect, or C, recover?

84
00:05:48,434 --> 00:05:49,261
Your answer?

85
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:59,746
Prevent, detect, recover. The answer is B.
The template's action table asks for the

86
00:05:59,786 --> 00:06:01,185
type of each control.

87
00:06:01,525 --> 00:06:06,385
A ruleset, GitHub's named list of rules
for chosen branches, that refuses a forced

88
00:06:06,425 --> 00:06:11,106
push prevents. The release job's ancestry
test detects: the rewrite had already

89
00:06:11,146 --> 00:06:16,126
happened, and the job noticed before
deployment. A backup ref, a deployment tag that

90
00:06:16,166 --> 00:06:21,026
names the running commit, or a clone that
hasn't fetched is what lets you recover.

91
00:06:21,066 --> 00:06:25,986
A good set of actions has at least a prevent
and a detect, because every prevention

92
00:06:26,026 --> 00:06:26,763
has a bypass.

93
00:06:27,103 --> 00:06:32,080
Prefer one strong control to five weak ones.
For the rewritten production branch the

94
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:36,620
list could have ten items. The one that
matters is a checkbox on the server.

95
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:39,548
"Engineers will take more
care" is not a control.

96
00:06:39,888 --> 00:06:45,177
When not to use any of this. Don't run an
incident process for a SEV 4, the lowest

97
00:06:45,217 --> 00:06:50,181
level. One person's local reset needs
ten minutes of help and one note, not a

98
00:06:50,221 --> 00:06:51,676
channel and a document.

99
00:06:53,516 --> 00:06:57,701
Three pictures help. For the summary,
think of a pilot's announcement after

100
00:06:57,741 --> 00:07:02,433
turbulence. It has four sentences: what
happened, why, that the aircraft is fine and

101
00:07:02,473 --> 00:07:06,996
how the crew knows, and what will be done
differently for the rest of the flight.

102
00:07:07,036 --> 00:07:11,560
Nobody in the cabin wants the control
inputs. And a pilot who says "everything is

103
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:16,123
checked" before the check is done has spent
the passengers' trust for the rest of

104
00:07:16,163 --> 00:07:16,743
the flight.

105
00:07:17,083 --> 00:07:20,726
Where the picture breaks: passengers
can't act on what they hear.

106
00:07:20,766 --> 00:07:25,372
A CTO can and will. The fourth sentence is
a request for a decision, with an owner

107
00:07:25,412 --> 00:07:26,336
and a date in it.

108
00:07:26,676 --> 00:07:31,665
For blamelessness, think of the next incident.
The person who caused this one holds

109
00:07:31,705 --> 00:07:34,998
the evidence for it, and will
be near the next one too.

110
00:07:35,038 --> 00:07:39,785
Whatever happens to them now decides
whether the next reflog is pasted into the

111
00:07:39,825 --> 00:07:41,542
channel or quietly collected.

112
00:07:41,882 --> 00:07:47,201
For controls, the ranking is a ladder again,
and this time you climb as high as you

113
00:07:47,241 --> 00:07:51,656
can. The ladder of video 184 said:
take the lowest rung that repairs.

114
00:07:51,696 --> 00:07:54,949
This one says: take the
highest rung that prevents.

115
00:07:56,789 --> 00:08:01,432
Look for four things. The subject line
carries status, severity and impact.

116
00:08:01,472 --> 00:08:06,551
The root cause names both layers: "Git
allows this; our GitHub rules did not block

117
00:08:06,591 --> 00:08:09,860
it". The third part has the
words "Not yet verified".

118
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:11,733
And there is no name anywhere.

119
00:08:12,073 --> 00:08:16,954
Three lines carry most of the weight. The
timeline has a third column: the source of

120
00:08:16,994 --> 00:08:21,699
the fact. "Contributing conditions" is
where blamelessness is done: what made the

121
00:08:21,739 --> 00:08:26,326
action look right. And the last column
of "Actions": how we will know it works.

122
00:08:28,166 --> 00:08:32,014
The deployment. When was the running
commit deployed, and which commit is it?

123
00:08:32,354 --> 00:08:38,115
Timeline line one: 10:14, plus 05:30, the
release job tagged 3277739 as deployed.

124
00:08:38,155 --> 00:08:43,916
Source: the annotated tag, a named pointer
to a commit that records who tagged it

125
00:08:43,956 --> 00:08:49,789
and when, with its tagger date and its
message. The tag is a witness that no later

126
00:08:49,829 --> 00:08:51,221
rewrite could alter.

127
00:08:51,561 --> 00:08:55,885
The rewrite. When was the branch rewritten,
and when was the rewrite published?

128
00:08:55,925 --> 00:08:59,088
Predict which clone's reflogs
can answer. Say it out loud.

129
00:09:03,928 --> 00:09:09,289
The clone that did the rewriting. Two
more lines. 10:25: a rebase of production

130
00:09:09,329 --> 00:09:12,912
finished in one clone. 10:26:
the result was amended.

131
00:09:12,952 --> 00:09:18,039
Source: that clone's branch reflog.
10:27: the rewritten branch was pushed.

132
00:09:18,079 --> 00:09:23,234
Source: that clone's reflog of the
remote-tracking branch, "update by push".

133
00:09:23,274 --> 00:09:28,772
In the document these lines say "a rebase
finished in a developer's clone", not a

134
00:09:28,812 --> 00:09:29,113
name.

135
00:09:29,453 --> 00:09:34,783
The adoption. When did a second clone adopt
the rewrite, and when did new work land

136
00:09:34,823 --> 00:09:35,171
on it?

137
00:09:35,511 --> 00:09:39,437
10:28: a second clone fetched
and saw a forced update.

138
00:09:39,477 --> 00:09:45,386
10:29: its branch was reset to the server's.
10:30: a new commit was made on top.

139
00:09:45,426 --> 00:09:51,262
10:31: that commit was pushed. Four lines,
four sources, from two reflogs of the

140
00:09:51,302 --> 00:09:52,216
second clone.

141
00:09:52,556 --> 00:09:53,244
The detection.

142
00:09:53,584 --> 00:09:59,054
10:33: our own clone first saw the forced
update. In real life the detection line is

143
00:09:59,094 --> 00:10:02,661
the time of the alert: here,
the release job's refusal.

144
00:10:03,001 --> 00:10:07,832
Now read the timeline as the postmortem
would. How long was it from the publication

145
00:10:07,872 --> 00:10:11,705
of the rewrite to our detection?
Work it out, and say it out loud.

146
00:10:16,545 --> 00:10:21,408
Six minutes. From 10:27 to the restore,
the server's branch pointed at a rewritten

147
00:10:21,448 --> 00:10:24,697
history: that's the window
of the summary's first part.

148
00:10:24,737 --> 00:10:29,719
Between the publication at 10:27 and the
detection, six minutes passed, and in those

149
00:10:29,759 --> 00:10:34,024
six minutes a second clone adopted
the rewrite and shipped on top of it.

150
00:10:34,064 --> 00:10:39,047
That interval is the "Detection" section,
and it's the argument for the control: the

151
00:10:39,087 --> 00:10:42,037
earlier the server refuses,
the shorter this list.

152
00:10:42,377 --> 00:10:46,208
Notice what the sandbox let
us do that real life doesn't.

153
00:10:46,248 --> 00:10:51,708
We read two colleagues' reflogs directly.
In real life you ask a colleague to run

154
00:10:51,748 --> 00:10:55,647
git reflog show --date=iso
origin/production and paste it.

155
00:10:55,687 --> 00:11:00,740
Whether they do depends on what happened
to the last person who pasted one.

156
00:11:01,080 --> 00:11:06,250
Now the four questions, against this timeline.
The layer that could have refused is

157
00:11:06,290 --> 00:11:10,957
the platform: the push at 10:27 was a
forced update of a production branch.

158
00:11:10,997 --> 00:11:15,979
The strongest control there is a ruleset
that blocks force pushes, with an empty

159
00:11:16,019 --> 00:11:21,189
bypass list. Its type is prevent. And the
detect control already exists and worked:

160
00:11:21,229 --> 00:11:23,197
the release job's ancestry test.

161
00:11:25,037 --> 00:11:26,685
Five mistakes to watch for.

162
00:11:27,025 --> 00:11:31,488
Answering the CTO with commands. Root
cause: the summary is written from the

163
00:11:31,528 --> 00:11:36,464
engineer's experience of the incident instead
of from the three decisions its reader

164
00:11:36,504 --> 00:11:37,175
has to make.

165
00:11:37,515 --> 00:11:40,391
Writing "verified" for
something that was inferred.

166
00:11:40,431 --> 00:11:45,195
Root cause: the check was not run, and the
claim was made to make the incident sound

167
00:11:45,235 --> 00:11:45,595
closed.

168
00:11:45,935 --> 00:11:50,546
A timeline without sources. Root cause:
times were taken from memory or chat, so

169
00:11:50,586 --> 00:11:53,510
they cannot be reconciled
when two accounts differ.

170
00:11:53,850 --> 00:11:58,539
A postmortem that names a person as the
cause. Root cause: it explains who acted

171
00:11:58,579 --> 00:12:03,267
instead of why the action looked right
and why nothing stopped it, so it changes

172
00:12:03,307 --> 00:12:06,695
nothing in the system and
teaches people to hide evidence.

173
00:12:07,035 --> 00:12:11,781
"We will be more careful" as the action.
Root cause: no layer was identified that

174
00:12:11,821 --> 00:12:16,684
could have refused the action, so there is
nothing that still works when someone is

175
00:12:16,724 --> 00:12:18,161
new, tired or in a hurry.

176
00:12:20,001 --> 00:12:25,007
Now, out of the lab. After the rewritten
production branch of a billing service has

177
00:12:25,047 --> 00:12:30,053
been restored, the engineer who led the
recovery writes the summary before she goes

178
00:12:30,093 --> 00:12:34,978
home. It's the slide you saw: four parts,
one screen, with one sentence that says

179
00:12:35,018 --> 00:12:38,686
what is not yet verified, the
CI caches, and when it will be.

180
00:12:39,026 --> 00:12:43,316
The postmortem follows within three
days. She builds the timeline from two

181
00:12:43,356 --> 00:12:48,230
colleagues' reflogs, which they paste without
hesitation, because the first sentence

182
00:12:48,270 --> 00:12:52,969
she said to them on the day was "nobody
is in trouble; I need your reflogs before

183
00:12:53,009 --> 00:12:57,825
you fetch again". Under "Contributing
conditions" she writes what made the clean-up

184
00:12:57,865 --> 00:13:02,739
look right: the branch had accumulated fixup
commits, the developer had been told to

185
00:13:02,779 --> 00:13:07,654
keep history tidy, and nothing distinguished
production from any other branch at the

186
00:13:07,694 --> 00:13:08,765
moment of the push.

187
00:13:09,105 --> 00:13:13,693
Under "What went well": an ancestry check
in the release job that somebody added two

188
00:13:13,733 --> 00:13:14,464
years earlier.

189
00:13:14,804 --> 00:13:19,638
The action table has two rows. A ruleset
on production, type prevent, owner the

190
00:13:19,678 --> 00:13:24,696
platform team, active the same day, verified
by an attempted forced push in a test

191
00:13:24,736 --> 00:13:29,693
repository with the same ruleset. And an
alert on the release job's refusal, type

192
00:13:29,733 --> 00:13:33,456
detect, so that the next refusal
reaches a person in minutes.

193
00:13:33,496 --> 00:13:35,616
She resists adding eight more rows.

194
00:13:37,456 --> 00:13:42,685
Your turn. Do Lab 38.1, "A
timeline and a CTO summary", in

195
00:13:42,725 --> 00:13:46,682
lab-manual/m38-communication-postmortems.md.

196
00:13:47,022 --> 00:13:51,791
Before you run a command, list the events
you expect the timeline to contain and,

197
00:13:51,831 --> 00:13:56,303
for each, predict which repository's
reflog or which tag will be its source.

198
00:13:56,343 --> 00:13:59,390
Then build the timeline with
a source on every line.

199
00:13:59,430 --> 00:14:04,317
Write the four-part summary on one screen,
mark each statement in the third part as

200
00:14:04,357 --> 00:14:08,948
observed or inferred, and include one
sentence that begins "Not yet verified".

201
00:14:08,988 --> 00:14:12,985
Then say the four parts aloud, one
sentence each, in under a minute.

202
00:14:13,325 --> 00:14:18,067
The challenge is Lab 38.2, "A blameless
postmortem", in the same file.

203
00:14:18,107 --> 00:14:22,371
Apply the test: replace every
name by a role and read it again.

204
00:14:24,211 --> 00:14:27,167
Read the question, then answer
out loud, in under two minutes.

205
00:14:32,007 --> 00:14:36,767
A strong answer gives the four parts in
order and, for each, the question it answers

206
00:14:36,807 --> 00:14:41,454
for the reader, not only its title. It
gives the rules that make each part usable:

207
00:14:41,494 --> 00:14:46,197
impact first and in business terms, the
layer named in the root cause, an owner and

208
00:14:46,237 --> 00:14:47,625
a date in the prevention.

209
00:14:47,965 --> 00:14:52,424
For the third part it states the rule and
its positive counterpart: what you do

210
00:14:52,464 --> 00:14:56,865
write when a check hasn't been run yet.
If you add when the first version of a

